module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.AdicCompletion.Basic
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Trunc
public import Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Eval
import Sheaf.RingTheory.PowerSeriesUniversalPropertyComplete

/-!
# Evaluation of power series in an adically complete ring

Source: Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tags 07NL and 07NN, proofs, the universal property
of power series rings (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-power-series-adic-evaluation).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace MvPowerSeries

/-- (blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-power-series-adic-evaluation) The universal property used in
Stacks Project, More on Algebra, Tags 07NL and 07NN: evaluation at finitely many elements
of an ideal of an adically complete ring is the unique continuous ring map with the
prescribed constants and variables. Its value is uniquely characterized by its reductions.
Here `F.truncTotal N` retains exactly the coefficients of total degree less than `N`, so
`MvPolynomial.eval₂ α b (F.truncTotal N)` is the finite sum of `α(c_ν) * b^ν` in the node.

Proof:
1. Apply lem-weil-pbc-power-series-universal-property-complete with coefficient ring `C`,
   complete ring `T`, ideal `I`, coefficient map `α`, and variables `b`. This gives existence,
   the prescribed constants and variables, the reduction formula, and uniqueness of the
   element with these reductions. It also gives uniqueness among all such ring maps,
   hence among the continuous ones.
2. An element of the `M`-th power of the ideal generated by the variables is a finite sum
   of monomials of total degree `M` times series. The coefficientwise product formula
   of def-weil-pbc-multivariate-power-series shows that each summand has zero coefficients
   of degree less than `M`. For no variables the ideal is zero, with the same conclusion.
3. For `N ≥ 1`, apply the reduction formula to an element of the `N`-th ideal power:
   its truncation below degree `N` is zero by step 2, so its image belongs to `I ^ N`.
   For `N = 0` the target ideal is the whole ring. Thus `M = N` gives continuity.
   Together with step 1 this proves every assertion. -/
theorem exists_unique_adic_evaluation {C : Type u} {T : Type v}
    [CommRing C] [CommRing T] (α : C →+* T) (I : Ideal T) [IsAdicComplete I T]
    (n : ℕ) (b : Fin n → T) (hb : ∀ i : Fin n, b i ∈ I) :
    ∃ E : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) C →+* T,
      (∀ c : C, E (MvPowerSeries.C c) = α c) ∧
      (∀ i : Fin n, E (X i) = b i) ∧
      (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ M : ℕ,
        ∀ F ∈ (Ideal.span (Set.range (X : Fin n → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) C))) ^ M,
          E F ∈ I ^ N) ∧
      (∀ E' : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) C →+* T,
        (∀ c : C, E' (MvPowerSeries.C c) = α c) →
        (∀ i : Fin n, E' (X i) = b i) →
        (∀ N : ℕ, ∃ M : ℕ,
          ∀ F ∈ (Ideal.span (Set.range (X : Fin n → MvPowerSeries (Fin n) C))) ^ M,
            E' F ∈ I ^ N) →
        E' = E) ∧
      (∀ (F : MvPowerSeries (Fin n) C) (t : T),
        (∀ N : ℕ, 1 ≤ N →
          Ideal.Quotient.mk (I ^ N) t =
            Ideal.Quotient.mk (I ^ N) (MvPolynomial.eval₂ α b (F.truncTotal N))) ↔
        t = E F) := by
  sorry

end MvPowerSeries
